  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 243 AM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Slow moving surface and upper level low pressure\nsystems remain over the region, keeping broad cyclonic flow in \nplace. This will lead to continued scattered showers, especially\nacross the higher terrain as the upper level low gradually \nshifts away from the region. This cyclonic flow will also lead \nto some lake effect showers off Lake Ontario, with increased \nshower chances expected across northern New York today and into \nTuesday. Temperatures will be on the cool side, with highs only \nclimbing into the 70s, with some locations in the Adirondacks \nstruggling to climb out of the upper 60s. A brief period of \nridging will build into the region for Wednesday, bringing some \ndrier weather conditions for the middle of the week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: An upper-level trough builds in on Thursday and \nFriday, bringing some showers and thunderstorms. A northern-\nstream surface low should track northeast out of the Great Lakes\nRegion and into Quebec during this time, though its center will\npass well too far to the north to have any direct impacts. \nHowever, as is typical for incoming upper level lows, a series \nof surface fronts/troughs should move through Thursday and \nThursday night, bringing multiple rounds of showers and \nthunderstorms. Model guidance has continued to keep any possible\nsecondary low development and most of the tropical moisture out\nto sea, preventing the heaviest rain from reaching the region. \nWhile a couple strong to severe storms could be possible on \nThursday, the synoptic setup and ingredients favor another \nmarginal low CAPE/high shear setup, though the exact details \nwill be refined before then. During that period, GEFS/EPS/CMCE \ncombined probabilities of more than a half inch of rain are down\nbelow 20 percent, though the convective nature will lead to \nsome locally higher totals. Broad upper level troughing and \nassociated cyclonic flow should remain over the weekend, keeping\ntemperatures on the cooler side, but conditions should remain \nmostly dry. Highs should be in the 70s with low humidity, while \nlows should be in the 40s and 50s.",
